                "evidence": "LatticeCache 2.3 Pro costs **$45/month**. Overages don't start until you go past the included allowances below, and the **largest single item you can store is 5 MB**.\n\n**Included in the $45/month:**\n| Resource | Included | Overage rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| Replicated NVMe cache storage | 20 GB | $1.80 per extra GB-month |\n| Read ops | 50 million/month | $0.12 per extra million |\n| Write ops | 10 million/month | $0.60 per extra million |\n\n**Hard limits in 2.3:**\n- Largest single item (value): **5 MB**\n- Longest key: 1,024 bytes\n- Concurrent client connections: 2,000 per cache namespace\n\nWrites cost five times as much as reads per extra million. So if your workload writes a lot, write overages will probably be the part of the bill that grows fastest. That's my own reading of the rates, not something the pricing page says.\n\n**Source:** These numbers come from the research vault page [[LatticeCache]] (`wiki/LatticeCache.md`). It is based on LatticeCache's official pricing and limits page, which was updated 2026-11-02, and a copy of that page saved to the vault on 2026-11-04 (`raw/2026-11/2026-11-04 latticecache-2-3-pricing-limits`). The vault page was checked on 2026-11-04. Pricing pages count as fast-changing, so pages like this are trusted for 30 days, and this one is 3 days old. I didn't search the web or change the vault.\n\nCoverage: vault only, 1 page used. No web providers (Exa) or social search were used."
